      "id": 1,
      "text": "Fisher was named after Oliver Williams Fisher, a prominent lumberman who helped establish the town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fisher%2C_Louisiana",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Fisher was built between 1899 and 1901 by the Louisiana Long Leaf Lumber Company.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fisher%2C_Louisiana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The village remained a company town until 1966, when the sawmill was sold to Boise Cascade Corporation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fisher%2C_Louisiana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1979, the remaining company buildings were listed on the National Register of Historic Places as the Fisher Historic District.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fisher%2C_Louisiana",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Fisher Sawmill Days, an annual festival started in 1976, takes place the third weekend in May and celebrates turn‑of‑the‑last‑century lumbering.",
